<v SPEAKER_1>Andy, just to echo and expand that, it is true that if somebody watches one of these episodes, they may go, well, I never knew that film existed, or I haven't seen that film for a long time. Let me look at it. And then they see it through this different lens that Brent was talking about. But I want to tell you, it happens to us. I mean, yes, I have seen Close Encounters, for example, just used it as an example. I saw it when it first came out. I saw that that was in 77. I saw the 1980 Director's Cut, or not the Director's Cut, the Special Edition. Then in 97, I think, I saw the Director's Cut. And I've seen it a couple of times since then. And so I sat down to watch it again for our podcast preparation. And I saw it completely differently. I saw things that I had missed in all those previous times, or forgotten that I knew. And what I think is making it fascinating is that, okay, if you're on need to know with somebody who's got game like Coldheart and Dolan, you got to bring game on what's in the news right now. But what we're trying to do is unearth a kind of a different thing. The game for us is to say, well, if you take a look, one of our upcoming shows is about under unidentified submerged objects. So there's one way to look at that. That would be go straight to Richard Dolan because he wrote a book about it. He knows a lot about it. But for us, we don't go to the book for the episode, we go to the abyss. We go to other places where people have referenced this in a film in a television way. And we watch those things and ask ourselves, what are they saying here? What exactly is being floated as the main ideas of this film? And then because we know something about the UFO history, ask, well, okay, was this the first time anyone said this out loud in this film? And then has it found its way into ufology? Or was it in ufology? And the person who wrote it, the film, knew that stuff and incorporated into the film. So that's kind of where you're going with the chicken and egg thing too, because it's not always the same answer. Sometimes it's out in ufology. And let's face it, there's, you know, 12,000 plus members of the Writers Guild of the United States, let alone around the world. And these people are all looking for great ideas. And they may know something about UFOs now, because it's in the news, and they may write something about it. And that's certainly happened over the years. But then the larger question for us is, yeah, that's all true. And we're going to look into those things. But for us, in addition to starting Dark Skies that way, where we both knew a lot and we did some investigation, we had a lot of conversations, the truth of the matter is, it really lit our brains on fire to have this guy come to our party and say, I want to talk to you guys about your pilot episode, which no one is supposed to have seen, but we've seen it and we have notes. I mean, think about that.

